1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:04,716
Some questions deserve a model far bigger than this computer can hold.

2
00:00:05,184 --> 00:00:11,322
A seventy billion parameter model is over forty gigabytes before it answers a word.

3
00:00:11,894 --> 00:00:18,319
Windlass hauls it through memory one layer at a time, so an ordinary PC can run it.

4
00:00:18,803 --> 00:00:23,317
It is slow on purpose, and everything you see today runs live.

5
00:00:23,889 --> 00:00:28,725
Our demo model is a small three billion one, so you can watch it work.

6
00:00:29,475 --> 00:00:31,175
Here is how it works.

7
00:00:31,175 --> 00:00:33,850
Windlass reads a GGUF model file from your drive.

8
00:00:33,850 --> 00:00:37,656
It reads the header first, never the whole model.

9
00:00:37,656 --> 00:00:41,637
Everything stays on this one computer, on loopback.

10
00:00:42,337 --> 00:00:44,950
One layer at a time goes into memory.

11
00:00:44,950 --> 00:00:49,129
While layer N computes on the CPU, layer N plus one loads from disk.

12
00:00:49,129 --> 00:00:56,493
The keys and values cache stays in RAM, so a seventy billion model needs about two gigabytes plus that cache.

13
00:00:57,193 --> 00:01:01,052
Callers are the browser UI, or any OpenAI-style client on this PC.

14
00:01:01,052 --> 00:01:07,112
The server checks the Host and Origin of every request and keeps a first-in, first-out queue.

15
00:01:07,812 --> 00:01:12,303
A machine playbook measures speed and quality here and publishes them.

16
00:01:12,303 --> 00:01:16,250
A number never measured shows as null, not as a guess.

17
00:01:16,250 --> 00:01:19,657
The log holds ids, counts and timings, never your text.

18
00:01:20,357 --> 00:01:25,469
Fairlead, the model router, treats Windlass as one more local provider.

19
00:01:25,469 --> 00:01:30,884
The route is deep-local, reached by name only, so interactive tools never wait on it.

20
00:01:31,584 --> 00:01:34,034
GPU offload is planned, not built.

21
00:01:34,034 --> 00:01:36,544
Today the math runs on the CPU.

22
00:01:37,244 --> 00:01:45,099
And the guardrails: loopback only, no outgoing connections, no text in the log, and it never stops in the middle of a reply.

23
00:01:45,677 --> 00:01:47,915
Setup needs no admin rights and no install.

24
00:01:47,915 --> 00:01:58,031
First, info reads only the model's header: twenty-eight layers, three point four gigabytes, Q8 weights, and the Llama 3 chat template.

25
00:01:58,031 --> 00:02:00,136
Nothing is ever downloaded.

26
00:02:00,808 --> 00:02:04,606
Then serve, with a cache of zero so the layers really stream.

27
00:02:04,606 --> 00:02:15,856
The log explains each choice: two layer slots of point four gigabytes, no layers kept resident, thirty-two threads, and the model loaded in a fraction of a second.

28
00:02:16,556 --> 00:02:21,878
The overview shows the model, its measured speed, and the base URL any program can use.

29
00:02:21,878 --> 00:02:26,157
There is no key to paste: nothing here leaves the computer.

30
00:02:26,157 --> 00:02:34,262
Now a real task: a short watch log, and a request for two bullets, with a system prompt that says answer only from the log.

31
00:02:34,962 --> 00:02:36,667
The reply streams token by token.

32
00:02:36,667 --> 00:02:45,466
Every token costs a pass over all twenty-eight layers, so it is slow, about a second a token here, and a seventy billion model would take minutes.

33
00:02:45,466 --> 00:02:46,821
That is the trade.

34
00:02:47,521 --> 00:02:51,094
When it finishes, the page shows the first-token time and the counts.

35
00:02:51,094 --> 00:03:02,519
Now the proof of streaming: the model file is about thirty-two hundred megabytes, yet the engine's memory is about one gigabyte, because only a couple of layers sit in RAM at once.

36
00:03:03,269 --> 00:03:23,748
These numbers were measured on this computer, not estimated: about a second a reply token in streaming mode, a tenth of a second a prompt token, the CPU as the limit, a perplexity score, and a context recall of four of four planted facts.

37
00:03:23,748 --> 00:03:26,021
Anything never measured shows a dash.

38
00:03:26,721 --> 00:03:30,439
The Playbook page says why each setting was chosen.

39
00:03:30,439 --> 00:03:35,716
Our cache of zero overrides the playbook, as the serve log showed.

40
00:03:35,716 --> 00:03:43,515
For scale, the repo's own stress test measured a seventy billion model at about eight point seven seconds a token.

41
00:03:43,515 --> 00:03:44,935
That was not rerun here.

42
00:03:45,185 --> 00:03:45,915
Now Fairlead.

43
00:03:45,915 --> 00:03:56,388
We register Windlass as a local provider, a custom OpenAI-compatible one, with a timeout of an hour, because a layer pass can be long.

44
00:03:56,388 --> 00:04:00,008
Discover finds its one model and its context length.

45
00:04:00,008 --> 00:04:01,805
Fairlead needs no code for this.

46
00:04:02,505 --> 00:04:09,577
Then the route, deep-local-3b, with this one target, failover, and local fallback off.

47
00:04:09,577 --> 00:04:13,839
Fairlead's own page lists it beside the others.

48
00:04:13,839 --> 00:04:20,985
It is reached only by asking for it by name, never by auto or fast, so interactive tools never wait on it.

49
00:04:21,501 --> 00:04:25,137
Each app gets its own Fairlead client key.

50
00:04:25,137 --> 00:04:30,072
It is shown once, and masked here, because it is a live key.

51
00:04:30,958 --> 00:04:37,239
Now the real call, through Fairlead's address, asking for deep-local-3b.

52
00:04:37,239 --> 00:04:42,295
The model on this machine is working through its layers, so we skip ahead.

53
00:04:42,995 --> 00:04:54,202
The reply is the model's own, with its token counts and the time it took through Fairlead: about ninety seconds, because the model streams from disk.

54
00:04:54,202 --> 00:04:54,851
Trust details.

55
00:04:54,851 --> 00:05:03,019
A request with the wrong Host name, or an Origin from some web page, gets a four oh three, so no browser tab can quietly drive your model.

56
00:05:03,719 --> 00:05:07,535
Now a long reply is running, and eleven more requests arrive at once.

57
00:05:07,535 --> 00:05:19,177
The queue holds eight, so three are told the server is busy, a five oh three that Fairlead treats as a reason to fail over, and the rest wait their turn, first in, first out.

58
00:05:19,177 --> 00:05:21,384
Stop cancels the running reply.

59
00:05:21,938 --> 00:05:24,816
The reply stopped and the queue drained.

60
00:05:24,816 --> 00:05:34,140
And the check command proves the source makes no outgoing connections, starts no process and loads nothing dynamically, before it will even compile.

61
00:05:34,140 --> 00:05:35,224
The evidence trail.

62
00:05:35,224 --> 00:05:50,729
Fairlead's Activity shows the request: client Windlass, route deep-local-3b, served by the Windlass provider, a hundred and two tokens in, sixty-nine out, first token after eighty-nine seconds.

63
00:05:51,141 --> 00:05:56,162
And Windlass's own log shows request ids, token counts and timings.

64
00:05:56,162 --> 00:05:59,903
It never records what you asked or what it answered.

65
00:06:00,153 --> 00:06:03,732
One more idea, a concept that is not built and not tested.

66
00:06:03,732 --> 00:06:11,679
Take that seventy billion model: eighty layers, forty-two gigabytes, too big for any one phone.

67
00:06:12,379 --> 00:06:16,702
Now split the layers into slices and give each device a few.

68
00:06:16,702 --> 00:06:25,143
Fourteen phones with about three gigabytes each could hold the whole model, on the ship's own network, with no cloud.

69
00:06:25,540 --> 00:06:29,477
A token then travels from phone to phone, in order.

70
00:06:29,477 --> 00:06:36,439
Only a small vector moves between them, about sixteen kilobytes, while each phone keeps its own layers and its own cache.

71
00:06:36,439 --> 00:06:40,863
A hundred and twenty billion model would take roughly two dozen phones.

72
00:06:41,625 --> 00:06:46,889
In theory that fits Windlass, because layers are already handled one at a time.

73
00:06:46,889 --> 00:06:52,929
But nobody has tried it, and it is slow by design, since every token visits every phone.

74
00:06:53,261 --> 00:07:07,227
Here is what it would take, none of it built: an opt-in network mode, because Windlass is loopback only today, authenticated devices, a slice map, and a plan for a phone that drops out.

75
00:07:07,227 --> 00:07:08,706
Planned, not promised.

76
00:07:08,956 --> 00:07:12,038
Windlass is slow on purpose and honest about it.

77
00:07:12,830 --> 00:07:16,610
Everything you saw ran live on this computer, through Fairlead.

78
00:07:17,397 --> 00:07:20,630
Not built yet: GPU offload.

79
00:07:21,398 --> 00:07:25,603
Not measured yet: batching throughput on real models.

80
00:07:26,303 --> 00:07:31,097
And the seventy billion timings are the repo's, not rerun today.

81
00:07:31,972 --> 00:07:35,037
Slow, local, and verifiable.
